WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:02.840
Evidence storage deserves planning before an incident.

00:00:03.060 --> 00:00:13.860
Evidence drives, secure shares, object storage buckets, and case repositories should be selected with capacity, access control, encryption, retention, and transfer needs in mind.

00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:22.400
Evidence may contain personal data, business secrets, credentials, security architecture details, regulated information, or attacker artifacts.

00:00:22.620 --> 00:00:25.500
Treating evidence casually can create a second incident.

00:00:25.720 --> 00:00:31.560
Write blockers are commonly discussed in forensics because they help prevent changes to source media during acquisition.

00:00:31.780 --> 00:00:38.820
The practical idea is simple: when evidence should not be altered, use approved methods that reduce the chance of writing to the source.

00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:46.800
Not every collection scenario uses a physical write blocker, especially in cloud, SaaS, and live response contexts, but the principle still applies.

00:00:47.020 --> 00:00:51.980
Preserve first, work from copies, and document any unavoidable changes.

00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:55.800
Hash verification is one of the simplest ways to support integrity.

00:00:56.020 --> 00:01:03.540
By calculating a cryptographic hash at collection and later comparing it against the stored item, analysts can detect whether a file changed.

00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:09.520
Hashes do not explain what evidence means, and they do not prove every aspect of collection was perfect.

00:01:09.740 --> 00:01:13.580
They do provide a practical integrity check that belongs in the toolkit workflow.

00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:16.440
Secure transport is also part of the toolkit.

00:01:16.660 --> 00:01:23.380
Evidence may need to move from a remote site, endpoint, cloud export, or external partner into a controlled repository.

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:29.680
Transfers should protect confidentiality and integrity, and access should be limited to people with a legitimate need.

00:01:29.900 --> 00:01:35.100
Backups and retention rules should align with policy, legal guidance, and case requirements.

00:01:35.320 --> 00:01:38.040
The toolkit should make the safe path the easy path.
